Yes—two useful simplifications fit the agreed policy.

**1. Remove the broker-specific retry classification.**

In [shouldRetry](/private/tmp/cyspbot-queue-main.5MYBS0/workers/cyspbot-github-webhook-processor/src/worker.ts:65), delete the broker HTTP-status branch. What remains is:

```ts
function shouldRetry(error: unknown): boolean {
  if (error instanceof GitHubReactionError) {
    return error.retryable;
  }

  return true;
}
```

That makes every token-acquisition failure retry automatically while preserving existing GitHub handling. No new error categories or wrappers are needed. Keep the broker error type for sanitized diagnostics.

**2. Detect recovery work with one condition: DLQ backlog > 0.**

Cloudflare already owns retry exhaustion and transfer to the DLQ. We don’t need to reconstruct that from error logs, attempt counts, or Worker invocation failures. Those signals don’t prove a job reached the DLQ. [Cloudflare retry semantics](https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/batching-retries/)

Call the alert **“Webhook jobs require recovery”**, since exhausted GitHub failures share that queue. Use logs to diagnose the cause. Native Cloudflare email support for this metric still needs account verification.

Keep the per-message catch and GitHub retry delays—they preserve useful behavior. No additional job state, timestamp fields, or age checks are needed.

Current main is unchanged. These are recommendations; no implementation changes made.

